A stream's read and write are read and write, once: a signal that cuts
one short with no data moved is the stream's error, EINTR, as POSIX has
fgetc and fputc fail ("[EINTR] The read operation was terminated due to
the receipt of a signal, and no data was transferred"), and as glibc and
musl do. Bionic's calls them again, so the program never sees the signal
there: busybox's interactive sh, which reads its lines by fgets where its
input is no terminal, took the next line after SIGINT, then dropped it
for the interrupt.

--- a/libc/stdio/stdio.cpp
+++ b/libc/stdio/stdio.cpp
@@ -676,12 +676,12 @@
 
 int __sread(void* cookie, char* buf, int n) {
   FILE* fp = reinterpret_cast<FILE*>(cookie);
-  return TEMP_FAILURE_RETRY(read(fp->_file, buf, n));
+  return read(fp->_file, buf, n);
 }
 
 int __swrite(void* cookie, const char* buf, int n) {
   FILE* fp = reinterpret_cast<FILE*>(cookie);
-  return TEMP_FAILURE_RETRY(write(fp->_file, buf, n));
+  return write(fp->_file, buf, n);
 }
 
 off64_t __sseek64(void* cookie, off64_t offset, int whence) {
